sphinx — provenance note
=========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`taproot`/`musig2`/`dtls`/`falcon`/`slhdsa` modules, which are likewise
self-contained crypto/codec modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

sphinx — clean-room implementation from BOLT#4 ("Onion Routing Protocol",
`lightning/bolts`), covering: "Key Generation", "Pseudo Random Byte
Stream", "Packet Structure" (incl. the `payload` TLV format), "Shared
Secret", "Blinding Ephemeral Onion Keys", "Packet Construction", "Onion
Decryption", and "Filler Generation". BOLT#4 is a public, open Lightning
Network specification — not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine; see
CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source NOTICE entry is strictly
required for the spec citation alone. No reference implementation's source
(lnd, c-lightning/core-lightning, eclair, ldk, etc.) was ported or
consulted as a design reference for this module — it was built directly
from the BOLT#4 specification text.

BOLT#1 ("Base Protocol") is cited for the `bigsize` variable-length integer
encoding (`bigsize.zig`), also a public specification artifact.

The official BOLT#4 test vector (`kat_vectors.zig`) was fetched directly
from two files in the `lightning/bolts` repository, `master` branch, for
this pass:

  - `bolt04/onion-test.json`
    (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lightning/bolts/master/bolt04/onion-test.json)
  - `04-onion-routing.md`
    (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lightning/bolts/master/04-onion-routing.md),
    the "# Test Vector" section's worked error-message trace (source of the
    5 per-hop `shared_secret` values; the JSON file does not itself publish
    intermediate shared secrets).

These are public specification/test artifacts — the BOLT's own published
conformance vectors — not copied from any Lightning implementation's test
suite. The 5 `shared_secret` values transcribed from `04-onion-routing.md`
were additionally INDEPENDENTLY RE-DERIVED (not merely copied) for this
pass: recomputed from `session_key`/the 5 node `pubkey`s via a from-scratch
Python secp256k1 point-multiply + the BOLT#4 blinding-chain recipe (the
`ecdsa` PyPI package for the underlying curve arithmetic only — no
Lightning-specific or Sphinx-specific code of any kind), and matched
byte-for-byte against the spec's published values before being embedded
here. See `SPEC.md`'s design section for the verification log.

`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` (field/scalar arithmetic, point group law,
`basePoint`, `mul`, `fromSec1`/`toCompressedSec1`), `std.crypto.stream.
chacha.ChaCha20IETF`, `std.crypto.auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha256`, and
`std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256` are used via their public APIs (MIT, part of
Zig itself); see `core.zig`'s and `keyderive.zig`'s module doc comments for
the specific recon findings.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: complete.** The packet codec, key-derivation primitives,
`bigsize` codec, per-hop TLV framing, and the three crypto cores
(`core.deriveHopSecrets`/`.construct`/`.process`) are all implemented and
KAT-tested against the official BOLT#4 test vector — `construct`
reproduces the published 1366-byte onion packet byte-exact, and a full
5-hop construct → process round-trip passes. See `SPEC.md`'s
"TODO(fable) — done record" for the per-core verification detail.
